th the repo right we  have source the source code there no  James no secrets in the source code then  that code gets pulled in by the CI CD  system and the CI CD system has the  operational secrets in it one  operational secret is the service key  that serves a count key that is used to  deploy code to GCP the other operational  secret is a the name of a storage bucket  that a Google Cloud Storage bucket and  that is deployed or set as an  environment variable in the runtime  environment so the runtime environment  can read it what does the runtime  environment do with the name of storage  bucket well when it needs a secret like  the API key for the payment API it can  go and hit that read from that cloud  storage bucket and and get that runtime  secret and what's cool about this is  that now that reading of the runtime  secret is managed through I am  in GCP which means it can be really  locked down who can read it James can't  read it for example para can't read  maybe none of the developers can read it  maybe it's only the the CTO Tseng who  can read this or and the runtime  environment so setting up this  environment means operational secrets  runtime secrets separately let's talk  about how those operational secrets are  put into the CI CD system in gitlab it's  easy you just enter them in the web UI  this means there is no file sitting  around with these secrets as soon as you  have a file it's very dangerous because  somebody might accidentally check it in  write in this way somebody types them  into the web UI nobody saves them  anywhere once you type these in let's  see where they go next so if we look at  the particular variables we have here  the third one down is the service  account key this is a key associated  with a service account from I am and  it's been entered here in a  base64-encoded way that way if anyone  accidentally looks at it takes a  screenshot echoes it out to a terminal  leave they've not accidentally seen it  it's a nice security thro